# NWP git attributes.
#
# ── Append-only arc documents: union merge ────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHY. The rollback registry and decision log are APPEND-ONLY ledgers written by
# concurrent agents. Every agent appends its own rows at the end of the same
# table, so git sees two different insertions at the same line and calls it a
# conflict — even though the two edits do not disagree about anything. On
# 2026-08-02 this single file caused more merge conflicts than every other file
# in the tree combined: !313, !317, !320 and !332 all stalled on it, and !332's
# author independently reported "the concurrent-appender problem in that file is
# structural and will recur".
#
# It is not a real conflict, and resolving it by hand a fourth time would be
# repairing rather than fixing. `merge=union` (a git built-in, no driver to
# install) keeps BOTH sides' lines. For a ledger whose semantics are "these
# things happened", keeping both is not merely a safe resolution — it is the
# only correct one. Dropping either side would erase a recorded rollback path,
# which is precisely the row an operator reaches for mid-incident.
#
# WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE, AND WHY IT WOULD NOT BE OTHERWISE. Union merge is
# dangerous on files where duplicate lines change meaning. Here the danger is
# specific and already covered: two agents can independently allocate the same
# checkpoint id, and union merge would happily keep both — one id, two different
# restore commands, which the existing gate calls "worse than a missing row".
# scripts/ci/lint-rollback-registry-ids.sh (CI job `lint:registry-ids`) fails on
# a duplicate id. That gate is what earns this attribute: it turns a silent bad
# merge into a red pipeline. Do not extend `merge=union` to any other file
# without an equivalent gate on that file's uniqueness rule.
#
# The gate's own header says it "does not fix" the structural defect and only
# makes collisions impossible to merge — "the cheap half". This is the other
# half: the collisions stop being conflicts at all, while the gate keeps the
# genuine ones red.
#
# KNOWN LIMIT — an amended row, replayed by REBASE. Worth stating precisely,
# because the two git paths differ and the difference is the whole limitation:
#
#   MERGE  of a branch that added a row and later amended it  -> correct. Only
#          the final version lands; git compares endpoints, so the amendment is
#          simply part of that side's content.
#   REBASE of the same branch                                  -> duplicates it.
#          Each commit is replayed in turn: the "add" lands, then the "amend"
#          arrives as a change to a line union has already decided to keep, so
#          both the superseded and the final version survive under one id.
#
# The rebase path is the one that matters here — it is what GitLab's rebase
# button does and what draining the queue does by hand. Observed for real on
# !317 (2026-08-02): a checkpoint row added as "MR TBD" and amended to
# "MR !317" came through as both. Both tests below reproduce it by rebase for
# that reason; asserting it via merge would report no problem and prove nothing.
#
# The right resolution is to drop the SUPERSEDED DRAFT — the one case where
# deleting a registry line is correct, since it is a stale version of a row
# rather than a distinct checkpoint.
#
# The limit is acceptable because it is not silent: lint:registry-ids fails on
# the duplicated id, so this surfaces as a red pipeline, never as a quietly
# doubled ledger. Plain appends — the common case, and the one costing a
# conflict per MR — rebase cleanly.
#
# SCOPE. Only these two ledgers, matched under any arc directory.
docs/reports/**/rollback-registry.md   merge=union
docs/reports/**/decision-log.md        merge=union
